    "summary": "Adire cloth: composed of two even lengths of machine plain woven European cotton machine sewn together. The textile is decorated using the stitch-resist and tie-dye method. The producer has sewn the desired pattern into the textile before dyeing the textile indigo. The textile is divided into alternating stripes in the weft direction including narrow stripes which have been stitched into the fabric and spirals which have been created by tying many tiny circles into the fabric prior to dyeing. The textile is bordered by two rows of small circles which have been tied into the fabric prior to dyeing.This pattern is called AKETE &#039;caps&#039; or &#039;owner of caps&#039;.  The hem is machine sewn.",
